"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The surgical findings included a Gleason 4+5, pT3a stage, negative surgical margins, non-invasion of seminal vesicles, absence of lymphovascular invasion, and no lymph node metastasis. These favorable pathological features counterbalance the high preoperative PSA of 12 and the high CAPRA-S score of 6.0. The biopsy grade (Gleason 0+0) differs significantly from the prostatectomy specimen (Gleason 4+5), which introduces uncertainty regarding the true extent of the disease.\n\nThe nodal status is documented as negative (no lymph node metastasis), which is a positive prognostic factor. The primary uncertainty lies in the postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory, as this information is currently missing. Postoperative PSA levels and their trend are critical for assessing recurrence risk. Information regarding the postoperative PSA and its trajectory would significantly revise this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 85.63 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 68.50–102.76 months; its coverage has not been validated."
